1. Nf3 d5 (1... Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. g3 b6 { Black avoids d5 - we could try to punish it with d4, but it's more practical to transpose. Sooner or later they'll probably play c5 and d5. } { [%cal Bd7d5,Bc7c5] } 4. Bg2 Bb7 5. O-O Be7 6. b3 O-O 7. Bb2 c5 8. e3 d6 { Black plays passively. It's objectively best to go d4, but that's what black wants, so we'll keep it closed with d3 instead. } 9. Nc3 Nbd7 10. d3 a6 11. Ne1 Bxg2 12. Nxg2 Qc7 13. g4! h6 14. h4 Nh7 15. f4 { With g5 and a queen infiltration coming next (Qf3 likely) }) 2. c4 e6 3. g3 Nf6 (3... c6 4. Bg2 Nf6 (4... f5 5. O-O Nf6 6. d3 Bd6 (6... Be7 7. b3 { Black is completely passive - not even going for e5. 
Therefore, we have time to go Bb2 and Nd2 before e4. } { [%cal Rf8d6,Gf8e7,Gc1b2,Gb1d2,Ge2e4,Gd1c2] } 7... O-O 8. Bb2 Nbd7 9. Nbd2 Bd6 10. e4 fxe4 11. dxe4 dxe4 12. Ng5 Nc5 13. Ndxe4 { [%cal Gc5e4,Gg5e4,Bf6e4,Bg2e4] }) 7. Nc3 O-O 8. cxd5 exd5 9. e4 fxe4 10. dxe4 dxe4 11. Ng5 Bf5 12. Ncxe4 Nxe4 13. Nxe4 { If black avoids the trade with Be5, we have Qb3+ } { [%csl Gb7][%cal Gd6e5,Gd1b3,Bf5e4,Bg2e4,Gb3b7] }) 5. O-O Be7 (5... Bd6 6. d4 { As black in the mirrored version, we go d3 to avoid colle system theory. But here, we have pretty much nothing to fear as black hasn't got time for a meaningful e4 break or anything. } { [%csl Gd6,Gd4][%cal Re6e5,Rd4e5] } 6... O-O 7. Nbd2 Nbd7 8. Re1 Re8 (8... e5 9. cxd5 cxd5 10. dxe5 Nxe5 11. Nxe5 Bxe5 12. Nf3 Bc7 13. Be3 Bf5 14. Qb3) 9. e4 dxe4 10. Nxe4 Nxe4 11. Rxe4 Nf6 12. Re1 Qc7 { [%cal Gf3e5] } 13. Ne5 Nd7 14. Nd3 e5 15. Bf4!! { [%cal Re5f4,Re1e8,Be5e4,Bg2e4] }) 6. b3 { [%cal Gf8e7,Gb2b3] } 6... O-O 7. Bb2 Nbd7 8. d3 b6 9. e4 dxe4 10. dxe4 Nxe4 11. Nd4 { Most probable continuation, we'll end up a piece up: (11... Ndf6 12. Nxc6 Qc7 13. Nxe7+ Qxe7 14. Bxf6 Qxf6 15. Bxe4 Qxa1 16. Bxa8) } { [%csl Rd8,Re7,Gc6][%cal Gg2e4,Gd4c6] }) (3... dxc4 { [RARE]
This is should be pretty rare in our move order - considering black has already said no to the c-pawn from the start. } 4. Bg2 (4. Na3 { CONTINUE - might be more practical to transpose into Reti accepted (Na3) } 4... Bxa3 (4... Nf6 5. Nxc4 Be7 6. Bg2 O-O 7. O-O) 5. bxa3 Nf6 6. Bg2 O-O 7. Bb2 Nbd7 8. O-O Rb8 9. Qc2 b5 10. Nd4 { [%cal Gd4c6] } 10... Bb7 11. a4 Bxg2 12. Kxg2 { Next we'll play Nc6 or take b5.
Black's pawn structure will end up worse. } { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gd4c6,Ba4b5] }) 4... Nf6 (4... a6 { Black is being greedy and wants to hold on to the pawn... let them } 5. O-O b5 6. d3 cxd3 7. Ne5 { [%cal Gd3e2] } 7... Ra7 { c6 is also somewhat common, and slightly better: (7... c6 8. Nxc6 Qb6 9. Ne5 Bb7 10. Be3 Qc7 11. Qc1)
Taking e2 with tempo on the queen doesn't work: (7... dxe2 8. Qxd8+ Kxd8 9. Re1) } 8. Be3 c5 9. a4 b4 10. Nxd3 Nd7 11. Nd2 Bb7 12. Bxb7 Rxb7 13. Rc1 Rc7 14. a5 { Stopping a5 but more importantly making room for the queen to pin the knight. } { [%cal Ra6a5,Gd1a4,Ga4e8] } 14... Ngf6 15. Qa4 { We'll grab c5 next with the bishop: (15. Qa4 Be7 16. Bxc5 Bxc5 17. Rxc5 Rxc5 18. Nxc5 Qe7 19. Nd3) } { [%csl Gc5][%cal Ge3c5,Rd7c5,Ra4e8] }) (4... Nc6 5. O-O Nf6 6. Qa4 Bd7 7. Qxc4) (4... c6 5. O-O b5 6. a4 { [%cal Ga7a6] } (6. d3 cxd3 7. Ne5 Bb7 8. Nxd3 Nf6) 6... Bb7 7. Nc3 (7. d3 cxd3 8. Ne5 a6 { [%cal Gd1d3,Ge5d3] } 9. Qxd3 Qxd3 10. Nxd3 Nd7 11. axb5 { Next we'll trade the rooks and then get our other rook to the a-file:(11... axb5 12. Rxa8+ Bxa8 13. Bf4 Ngf6 14. Nc3 Nd5 15. Ra1 Bb7 16. Ra7) } { [%cal Ga1a8,Ga6b5,Bc1f4,Bb1c3,Bf1a1] }) (7. Ne1 a6 8. d3 cxd3 9. Nxd3 Nf6 10. Nd2 Be7 11. b4 O-O 12. Nb3 Nbd7 13. Na5) (7. Ne5 a6 8. axb5 axb5 9. Rxa8 Bxa8 10. b3 cxb3 11. Qxb3 Bd6 12. Bb2 Nf6 13. d4 O-O 14. Nc3) 7... b4 8. Ne4 c5 9. Ne5) (4... Nd7 5. Qc2 Nb6 6. a4 a5 7. Na3 Bxa3 8. Rxa3 Nf6 9. e4 O-O 10. O-O Bd7 11. d3 cxd3 { This is the most probable continuation... but it was more critical for black take a4 first: (1. Nf3 d5 2. c4 e6 3. g3 dxc4 4. Bg2 Nd7 5. Qc2 Nb6 6. a4 a5 7. Na3 Bxa3 8. Rxa3 Nf6 9. e4 O-O 10. O-O Bd7 11. d3 Bxa4 12. Rxa4 cxd3 13. Qb3 Nxa4 14. Qxa4 Qd7 15. Qc4 b5 16. Qc5 Nxe4 17. Qe3) which gets really messy. But it hasn't been played before, and Maia rates it as 0.7% probability. } { [%cal Gd7a4] } 12. Rxd3 Bxa4 13. b3 Bd7 14. e5 Nfd5 15. Rd4 { Stepping away from the fork - also enabling Rh4.
A key idea next is Ng5 with a mate threat.
Combined with Rh5 can get very spicy: (15... Bb5 16. Ng5 g6 17. Re1 Qe7 18. Rh4 h5 19. Rxh5) } { [%csl Rd3,Rc2][%cal Rd5b4,Gf3g5,Bd4h4,Bh7h5,Bh4h5,Bg7g6] }) 5. O-O Be7 6. Na3 O-O 7. Nxc4 c6 8. d4 Nbd7 9. Qc2 Nb6 10. Nce5 { [%cal Gb6d5] } 10... Nbd5 11. a3 { [%cal Rd5b4] } 11... Nd7 { [%cal Ge5c4] } 12. e4 Nxe5 13. Nxe5 Nf6 14. Rd1 { Either way the queen moves, Nc4 is a good continuation. } { [%cal Bd8b6,Gd8c7,Ge5c4] }) (3... c5 4. cxd5 exd5 5. d4 Nc6 6. Bg2 Nf6 7. O-O Be7 8. dxc5 Bxc5 9. Nc3 O-O 10. a3 d4 11. Na4 Be7 12. b4 { Likely continuations:
29%: (12... a6 13. Bb2 Bd7 (Black sets up a sneaky discovery. Against most other moves, we can capture on d4.) 14. Rc1 Rc8 15. Nc5)
21%: (12... b5 13. Nc5 Bxc5 14. bxc5 Bb7 15. Nxd4)
17%: (12... Bf5 13. Bb2 d3 14. Re1 dxe2 15. Qxe2 Re8 16. Qb5)
17%: (12... Bg4 13. b5 Bxf3 14. Bxf3 Ne5 15. Bxb7 Rb8 16. Bg2 Rxb5 17. Bb2) } { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gc1b2,Ba4c5] }) 4. Bg2 Be7 5. O-O O-O 6. b3 c5 (6... b6 7. Bb2 Bb7 8. e3 { Preparing Nc3 by stopping d4. 
The engine prefers cxd5, but this has better results practically. } 8... c5 9. Nc3 { d4 doesn't work as we have Nb5: (8. c5 9. Nc3 d4 10. exd4 cxd4 11. Nb5) } { [%cal Rd5d4,Ge3d4,Gc3b5] } 9... Nc6?! { The most common move, and a slight inaccuracy. The knight blocks the bishop and we get more control over d5. } { [%cal Gc4d5,Gb8c6,Rb8d7,Rd1e2] } (9... Nbd7 10. Qe2 { Black doesn't block the bishop with Nc6 - we avoid taking on d5. } { [%cal Rb8c6,Rc4d5,Gb8d7,Gd1e2] } 10... Rc8 11. d3 { Further protect c4 - now with the queen off the d-file, we'd prefer to take back with dxc4 and getting a rook to d1. } { [%cal Gd5c4,Gd3c4,Ba1d1] } 11... Qc7 { [%cal Gf3d2,Gd2e3] } 12. Nd2 { Stepping away to enable f4. } { [%cal Gf2f4] } 12... Rfd8 13. f4 { If black goes for the bishop trade, it can get tactical: (13... dxc4 14. dxc4 Bxg2 15. Qxg2 Nb8 16. g4 Nxg4 17. Qxg4 Rxd2 18. Nd5) } { [%cal Gd5c4,Gd3c4,Gb7g2,Be2g2,Bg3g4] }) (9... dxc4 10. bxc4 Nc6 11. Qe2 Qc7 12. d3 Rad8 13. Rad1 { Add protection to d3. } { [%csl Gd3,Ge2,Gd1][%cal Gc6b4] } 13... Rd7 14. Ne1 { To enable f4 - we don't want to block the rook with Nd2. } 14... Rfd8 15. f4) 10. cxd5 Nxd5 (10... exd5 11. d4 Rc8 12. Rc1 Re8 13. Re1 Bf8 14. Ne2 Ne4 15. Nf4 { Threatening to take on c5 and opening up the attack on d5.
If black deals with it, we can challenge black's knight on e4: (Ne7 16. dxc5 bxc5 17. Nd2) } { [%csl Gd5,Gd1][%cal Gd4c5,Gf4d5] }) 11. Nxd5 Qxd5 (11... exd5 12. d4 Bf6 13. Qd2 Rc8 14. Rfd1 c4 { This is most common, but black can also go for a big trade: (10. cxd5 Nxd5 11. Nxd5 exd5 12. d4 Bf6 13. Qd2 Rc8 14. Rfd1 cxd4 15. Nxd4 Nxd4 16. Bxd4 Bxd4 17. Qxd4 Rc7 18. e4) } 15. bxc4 dxc4 16. e4 { [%cal Gc6a5] } 16... b5 17. d5 { [%cal Gf6b2,Gd2b2,Bc6b8,Be4e5,Bd5d6,Bf6e7] }) 12. d4 Rfd8 13. Ne5 Qd6 14. Qf3 Nxe5 15. Qxb7 Nd3 16. Ba3 { In case of Nb4 - we trade.
In case of Rab8 - we take Qxa7. }) (6... d4 7. Bb2 { e3 might be better, but we want to transpose into some kind of benko. } 7... c5 8. b4 { And we have transposed into the line where black played c5 -> d4 }) 7. Bb2 d4 (7... Nc6 8. e3 b6 9. Nc3 { And we have transposed into a good b6 line where black blocks the bishop with their knight. Next we'll likely capture on d5. } { [%cal Gc8b7,Gc4d5] }) 8. b4!? { We actually get to transpose into the benko! } { [%cal Gd4d3,Gb1c3] } 8... Nc6 { Most common move (60%) even though it walks into... } (8... cxb4 9. Nxd4 { We prefer to take with the knight as we open up the bishop, even though it runs into e5. } 9... e5 10. Nc2 Nc6 11. a3 bxa3 { If black doesn't capture, we can.
b3 looks annoying but will probably be a target in the future: (11... b3 12. Ne3 Nd4 13. Nc3 a5 14. a4) } 12. Bxa3 Bxa3 13. Rxa3) (8... b6 9. Ne1 Nd5 10. cxd5 exd5 11. e3 { Might continue like: (11... dxe3 12. dxe3 Bb7 13. bxc5 bxc5 14. Nc3 Bf6 15. Rb1) }) (8... a5! { Black's best move, only played 7% of the time. } 9. b5 Nbd7 10. d3 e5 11. Nbd2 h6 12. Qc2 Re8 13. Rae1 { Next or soon we'll go for e3. }) (8... Re8! { [Rare 2%] } 9. bxc5 Bxc5 10. e3 e5 11. exd4 exd4 12. d3 Nc6 13. Nbd2 Bg4 { In case of Bf4 we can also go Nb3 to defend the d-pawn with tempo on the bishop. } 14. h3 Bh5 15. Nb3 { If black moves back to b6: (15... Bb6 16. c5 Bc7 17. Nbxd4 Nxd4 18. Bxd4 Bxf3 19. Bxf6 Bxd1 20. Bxd8)
If black protects with b6: (15... b6 16. g4 Bg6 17. Nxc5 bxc5 18. Ne1 Rc8 19. f4) } { [%cal Gc5b6,Gc4c5,Bb7b6,Bg3g4,Bb3c5,Bf3e1,Bf2f4] }) 9. b5 Nb8 (9... Na5 10. d3 Rb8 11. Nbd2 b6 12. Nb3 Nb7 { If black trades, we'll recapture with the pawn to open up the rook: (9... Na5 10. d3 Rb8 11. Nbd2 b6 12. Nb3 Nxb3 13. axb3 Qc7 14. e3) } 13. Ne5 { Threatening the fork on c6 but more importantly blocking e5! } 13... Bd7 14. f4 Nd6 15. g4) 10. e3 dxe3 11. dxe3 Nbd7 12. a4 { If allowed, continue with a5.
